so 2020 has been quite there instead of  presenting a standard scare of the  bejesus out of you monster  let's give our minds and our adrenaline  a rest because isn't that what we all  need right now if the demonic knuckle of  e  is on the disgusting end of the monster  spectrum the kasaganaga sits on the  adorable  opposite end a sky deity from the  mythology of the toba peoples of south  america  the kasaganaga appears as a small hairy  creature with short legs  who fell out of the clouds and needs a  little human help to return to the sky  it has the ability to bring rain  lightning and thunder  and a history of helping the indigenous  people of south america  i'm dr emily zarka and this is monstrum  the kasuganaga hails from the grand  chaco region of south america  which includes modern day argentina  bolivia and paraguay  because it is spread across many  cultures and regions  it goes by a few names this diminutive  creature is genderless  and its physical appearance across  stories varies as widely as its name  but its most common and defining  characteristic is its ability to control  storms the kasaganaga resides in the  clouds and can be called on by a shaman  or pyoxanak for assistance  it can create thunder control the rain  and command lightning at will  sometimes even emitting lightning from  its mouth  this furry helper is described as having  a small head and a body covered with  long hair like an anteater  with four tiny feet and a long snout or  occasionally as a small multicolored  elephant  some modern depictions of the kasaganaga  add rainbow hair  which may just be a conflation between  it and another  non-human being from topo mythology the  komanalo  described as a rainbow snake that also  brings storms  a rainbow-haired anteater storm deity i  love it  as with many creatures whose origins are  in oral traditions  kasoganaga's name appearance and stories  are influenced by the language religion  and community of its various regions  topa spiritual practices integrate  animism  a world view that asserts that humans  non-human animals and non-living  entities  all have spirits or sentience and  influence the lives of people  part of that belief is that most  non-human entities are capable of  changing into whatever corporeal body  they wish  as if changing clothes meaning the  altering physical depictions of the  kasaganaga across  cultures are not seen as unusual but  most beliefs still  center around the same general narrative  it's a deity who fell from the sky and  needs human help to return  the origin story goes a man out hunting  heard a voice in a clearing  and came upon lightning the creature  spoke to the man  telling him it had fallen from the sky  and could not return to the clouds  the being implored the man for help  asking him to build a large fire so it  could use the smoke to rise back to the  heavens  the creature too small to climb onto the  fire itself  told the man to pick it up and place it  upon the fire  and after to run home immediately he  follows these instructions and when  lightning reaches the sky  a violent thunderstorm covers the  drought-stricken land with a heavy rain  a sign of the entity's gratitude  kasaganaga was so thankful it continued  to protect and watch over the man  communicating with him through the air  and giving him the power to heal people  the man became a shaman and the grateful  sky deity promised he could call upon it  at any time  and if anyone bothered him the  kasaganaga would strike his enemy with  lightning  and kill them so this idea that a human  could interact with a non-human deity  isn't an odd concept for the toba people  in animism non-human entities coexist  with humans  and although not all people can see or  interact with them  their general existence is agreed upon  the role of animism in everyday life of  the toba people  is further demonstrated in their  retelling of historical events  which often involves human and non-human  intervention  the indigenous toba people also known as  the guam  found their land infiltrated by jesuit  missionaries in the 17th century  and eventually part of the grand choco  region was declared  national territory of argentina in 1884.  that same year a military campaign  called the conquest of the chaco desert  led to violent conflict with the guam as  the argentine government  built settlements for further conquest  their pursuits were physical  spiritual and cultural many of the  stories of this period of colonization  include the indigenous people escaping  or even defeating the soldiers  because of communication with entities  one story tells of kasoganaga helping a  group of toba women  captured by the argentine military  during the campaign of occupation of  indigenous communities  six women were collecting carob fruit  when the military seized them  tied them up and forced them to walk  towards a prison camp  while the captors rode horses and  donkeys the women were bound and held  captive at the prison camp and through  communication with kasuganaga  who implored the women to escape that  night if they had  any hope of survival which they did  because we know the story today  for the quan people time is fluid and  history is not always recounted exactly  chronologically  and older oral traditions merge with  modern history  in recent years as the guam continue  their fight for rights to ancestral  territory  non-human figures including the  kasaganaga appear in the retelling of  these modern histories  according to one story an electrical  storm allowed the quan people to set up  roadblocks to prevent a hundred police  officers  from evicting a group of indigenous  people from a local school  the quan people say that the unrelenting  rain thunder and lightning  that afforded them protection and  camouflage was sent by kasaganaga  the storm reportedly ceased only when  both roadblocks were complete  as a creature who still has a supportive  role to play in the lives of the qualm  people today  this monster is different from many of  the other ones i've covered in a few  ways  and since i frequently research monsters  who are destructive vindictive and  downright evil  i find the helpful kasaganaga to be a  welcome relief  and a pleasant and important reminder  that even in 2020  not all monsters are bad  i also would like to hug it but i would  definitely ask permission first  if only there was a plush version of it
 

